  "assets": [
    {
      "assetId": "math.prob.bayes-theorem/learning_objective/1",
      "kind": "learning_objective",
      "conceptId": "math.prob.bayes-theorem",
      "title": "Learning Objective",
      "content": "The student states Bayes' theorem P(B|A)=P(A|B)P(B)/P(A); identifies the roles of prior P(B), likelihood P(A|B), posterior P(B|A), and normalising constant P(A); computes P(A) using the law of total probability when needed; applies Bayes' theorem to update beliefs given evidence in medical testing, spam filtering, and base-rate neglect scenarios; and distinguishes the posterior from the likelihood (avoiding the classic confusion of P(disease|test+) with P(test+|disease)).",
      "localKey": null,
      "language": "en",
      "refs": [],
      "span": {
        "file": "docs/curriculum/blueprints/math.prob.bayes-theorem.md",
        "startLine": 18,
        "endLine": 21
      }
    },
    {
      "assetId": "math.prob.bayes-theorem/misconception/MC-1",
      "kind": "misconception",
      "conceptId": "math.prob.bayes-theorem",
      "title": "POSTERIOR-EQUALS-LIKELIHOOD",
      "content": "| MC-1 | POSTERIOR-EQUALS-LIKELIHOOD | Student writes P(B|A)=P(A|B) or computes P(disease|test+)=P(test+|disease)=sensitivity; ignores the prior and base rate entirely | Type 5 — instruction-induced (sensitivity is the headline number reported for medical tests; students compute \"how likely is a positive test given disease\" and reverse it without Bayes) |",
      "localKey": "MC-1",
      "language": "en",
      "refs": [
        "MC-1"
      ],
      "span": {
        "file": "docs/curriculum/blueprints/math.prob.bayes-theorem.md",
        "startLine": 28,
        "endLine": 28
      }
    },
    {
      "assetId": "math.prob.bayes-theorem/misconception/MC-2",
      "kind": "misconception",
      "conceptId": "math.prob.bayes-theorem",
      "title": "BASE-RATE-NEGLECT",
      "content": "| MC-2 | BASE-RATE-NEGLECT | Student focuses only on sensitivity and specificity, ignores P(disease); computes a posterior far too high when the prior is very small (e.g. rare disease scenario) | Type 3 — language contamination (\"the test is 95% accurate\" implies P(disease|test+)≈0.95 in everyday language, conflating test accuracy with posterior probability) |",
      "localKey": "MC-2",
      "language": "en",
      "refs": [
        "MC-2"
      ],
      "span": {
        "file": "docs/curriculum/blueprints/math.prob.bayes-theorem.md",
        "startLine": 29,
        "endLine": 29
      }
    },
    {
      "assetId": "math.prob.bayes-theorem/misconception/MC-3",
      "kind": "misconception",
      "conceptId": "math.prob.bayes-theorem",
      "title": "BAYES-ONLY-WORKS-FOR-TWO-HYPOTHESES",
      "content": "| MC-3 | BAYES-ONLY-WORKS-FOR-TWO-HYPOTHESES | Student applies the formula only when B is binary (disease/no disease); doesn't extend to partitions B₁,…,Bₙ with the generalised form P(Bᵢ|A)=P(A|Bᵢ)P(Bᵢ)/∑ⱼP(A|Bⱼ)P(Bⱼ) | Type 5 — instruction-induced (all introductory examples use exactly two hypotheses; the general partition form is rarely shown) |",
      "localKey": "MC-3",
      "language": "en",
      "refs": [
        "MC-3"
      ],
      "span": {
        "file": "docs/curriculum/blueprints/math.prob.bayes-theorem.md",
        "startLine": 30,
        "endLine": 30
      }
    },
